    rubric: [['Sets interval from manufacturer recommendations and high-risk status (no AEM for life-support), plus risk assessment', ['manufacturer', 'high-risk', 'life support', 'interval', 'risk']], ['Lists inspection contents (visual, battery, self-test, energy output, charge time, sync, pacing, ECG, alarms, electrical safety)', ['energy', 'charge time', 'battery', 'synchron', 'pacing', 'electrical safety', 'visual']], ['Names test equipment and calibration (defibrillator analyzer with 50 Ω load, safety analyzer; traceable calibration)', ['defibrillator analyzer', '50', 'safety analyzer', 'traceable', 'nist']], ['Describes documentation and metrics (asset records, work orders, PM completion 100%, uptime, MTBF, repeat failures, recalls)', ['cmms', 'work order', 'completion', 'uptime', 'mtbf', 'recall']]],
    model: 'Defibrillators are high-risk, life-support equipment, so their inspection interval and procedures follow the manufacturer’s recommendations rather than an alternate schedule, confirmed by a risk assessment and accreditation requirements. Each new unit would receive an incoming inspection, an asset tag and a CMMS record with model, serial number, software version, location and risk class. A scheduled inspection would include a visual check of the case, cables, pads and connectors, a review of self-test logs, battery condition and replacement date, energy delivery accuracy at several settings and charge time to maximum energy using a defibrillator analyzer with a 50-ohm load, synchronized cardioversion timing, pacing output, ECG display and alarms, and an electrical safety test with a safety analyzer; all test equipment would be calibrated with traceability to NIST. Results, including measured values, pass or fail status, parts replaced and technician name, would be recorded on work orders in the CMMS, which also schedules the next inspection and tracks recalls. To show the program works I would track on-time PM completion (targeting 100% for life-support equipment), uptime, mean time between failures, repeat failures and recall completion.',
